ng to be reversed it's carrying  on it's great for the gps they love it  and they don't want to turn the clock  back um on triage  so what was introduced as an emergency  system for the pandemic is now the new  normal  it's now the new normal receding in  response to public opposition i think  but it's still the new normal yes right  now you see in your article that gps  despite um seeing fewer face-to-face  patients never before are still quite  unhappy and very few of them are working  in five-day weeks and  i i don't quite understand what the  problem is if they're you know the pay  is not entirely ungenerous the hours  from what you say are evolved fewer  face-to-face contacts never before so  what's is why there is so many gps  saying but they intend to leave general  practice within a couple of years  surveys regularly say but they're very  very unhappy they find the pressure  untenable it seems the system isn't  working for them as well as the patients  that's right they um say that they're  suffering from burnout and as i  explained in my article i don't quite  understand that because i was a surgeon  in the nhs with a cancer surgeon for 33  years i don't remember seeing any  colleagues being burned out  but the real problem um in  general practice phraser is part-time  working um so if we know that 58 percent  of gps work part-time  they work three days or less and that is  absolutely atrocious  so there are 70 000 gps on the gmc gp  register  40  000 of those work part time three days  or less and 25 000 of those work two  days or less  so this is the point that's never made  by the royal college of gps or by the  bma the reason that patients have  difficulty finding access to gps  is  not because primarily we haven't got  enough gps but because the gps we've got  are all working part-time  and there are horrible consequences of  that and i think  that  the iran college of gps and the bma  should actually apologize to the public  and make it clear  that on